{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfjnkUUi6KNpcCQgEd3z9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PvQLmEtWGPSkN2cu8ze1dt","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment3/model_loop; D=iter_0/candidate_2; ls $D; cat > $D/hypothesis.md << 'EOF'\nPeople judge randomness by exemplar categorisation in a structural code: they remember a few \"designed\" sequences — a streak, perfect alternation, and short repeated patterns (such as HHTT…, HHT…, HHHT…) — stored not as exact strings of heads and tails but as their rhythm of repeats and switches, so a remembered pattern matches a new sequence at whatever point it starts and whichever face it begins with. A sequence's similarity to each remembered design falls off exponentially with the share of its repeat/switch steps that break that design at the best alignment, and the sequence is classed as \"random\" to the extent this summed similarity to the designs is small against a fixed pull of the random category (each kind of design — streak, alternation, repeated motif — has its own fitted memorability); the sequence more likely to be classed random is chosen, with people differing in how decisively they act on that classification and in a habitual side preference at the response stage.\nEOF\necho structural_exemplar_designs > $D/model_name.txt"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4727,"cache_read_input_tokens":70855,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4727,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d14f6578-2fc4-47d4-ae38-57011b1b755a","uuid":"0f3af772-1cf4-4be2-b5ef-00a